1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,520
Every night, small sellers go live, and their chat looks like this.

2
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:08,140
Somebody types mine. Then four more people do.

3
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:14,060
The first one gets it. And then, they never pay.

4
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:20,300
The item sits on hold, and the next buyer has already left.

5
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:27,420
In the US, people bought fourteen point six billion dollars of goods on live video in 2025.

6
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:32,760
This is Dibs. It runs the sale, so the seller can keep selling.

7
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:43,320
Hold an item up and say what it is. Lot three, leather bag, thirty five.

8
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:47,880
Dibs hears the price, looks at the camera, and writes the listing.

9
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:57,680
Buyers claim it however they talk, in slang, emoji, Spanish or Hindi.

10
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,783
Clear claims are decided by plain rules, in the order the server heard them.

11
00:01:02,783 --> 00:01:07,440
Gemini reads the messy ones, and answers each buyer in their own language.

12
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:16,720
Already live on YouTube? Paste the link. Dibs reads that chat too, holds the item, and answers price questions while you keep talking.

13
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:25,880
Whoever was first gets a PayPal checkout on their phone, and a clock.

14
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:34,240
Jake doesn't pay. When his time runs out, the jacket moves to the next person in line, and Lucia hears about it in Spanish.

15
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:37,920
If Jake tries to pay late, Dibs refuses the capture.

16
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:41,240
Nobody pays for something that went to someone else.

17
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:52,480
Everything a buyer holds goes into one PayPal order, built with the PayPal server SDK.

18
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:59,320
Lucia pays. PayPal captures it, the webhook signature checks out, and the board turns green.

19
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:11,131
After the show, the seller just asks. Who hasn't paid?

20
00:02:11,131 --> 00:02:13,480
Refund fifteen dollars on lot one.

21
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:18,650
The co-host proposes it, and nothing moves until the seller taps confirm.

22
00:02:18,650 --> 00:02:21,640
Then PayPal's agent toolkit sends the refund.

23
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:29,640
All of this ran in the PayPal sandbox, with real orders, a real capture and a real refund.

24
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,560
There's one link for the seller and one for buyers, and nothing to install.

25
00:02:39,400 --> 00:02:43,400
Dibs. Sell it live, and get paid before the next item.
